WEBVTT

00:00:00.101 --> 00:00:05.152
This is WordEval: evaluating Word-native
operation fidelity in document editing,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.162
presented at NeurIPS twenty twenty-six.

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:13.659
Imagine asking AI agents to
edit your Word documents.

00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:19.565
Agents have begun to work inside Microsoft
Word: they open the file, make the edit,

00:00:19.744 --> 00:00:25.547
and save it. Here, two agents are asked
to turn two items into a numbered list.

00:00:26.428 --> 00:00:28.047
Both results look the same.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.688
Now add one item in the middle.

00:00:31.568 --> 00:00:36.249
In the left list, the new item gets no
number, and the old numbers stay fixed.

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:40.394
In the right list, Word numbers
it and updates the rest.

00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:42.996
Where does this difference come from?

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:48.086
The first agent typed the numbers
as characters, so Word sees ordinary

00:00:48.099 --> 00:00:52.002
paragraphs. The second
created a real Word list.

00:00:52.882 --> 00:00:55.777
So looking the same does not
show that an edit worked.

00:00:56.657 --> 00:01:01.951
Do AI agents actually produce the effect
we ask for, in a form Word recognizes?

00:01:03.845 --> 00:01:09.841
Here is a real case. The agent was asked to
add solid circle bullets to all paragraphs

00:01:09.854 --> 00:01:12.048
and set one point five line spacing.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.708
Its saved output and the
reference look identical.

00:01:16.588 --> 00:01:21.180
WordEval opens both files in Word and
reads the properties the request names.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.009
The agent typed a bullet character before
each line, so Word sees plain paragraphs,

00:01:27.175 --> 00:01:31.652
not a list. Line spacing
matches; the list does not.

00:01:32.532 --> 00:01:38.081
So the task fails. This is what
WordEval measures: operation fidelity.

00:01:40.001 --> 00:01:42.731
More generally, here is how WordEval works.

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:48.497
An agent receives a Word document and a
request, and edits it with its own tools.

00:01:49.377 --> 00:01:54.301
We judge only the saved document; a
missing or invalid file fails right away.

00:01:55.193 --> 00:02:00.053
The checker compares requested changes and
preserved parts with expected values from a

00:02:00.066 --> 00:02:03.408
reference document, the
input, or task metadata.

00:02:04.288 --> 00:02:08.178
The reference is made directly in
Word, and the agent never sees it.

00:02:09.071 --> 00:02:12.247
A task passes only when
all required checks pass.

00:02:14.141 --> 00:02:19.167
WordEval contains six hundred forty-two
tasks covering fifteen Word feature areas,

00:02:19.422 --> 00:02:23.274
including text, lists,
tables, charts, and shapes.

00:02:24.155 --> 00:02:26.259
Atomic tasks test one edit.

00:02:27.152 --> 00:02:32.076
Compositional tasks combine linked edits
in one document, asking whether edits that

00:02:32.088 --> 00:02:33.861
work alone still work together.

00:02:34.742 --> 00:02:39.614
Each combined edit links to a single-edit
task using the same command, helping us

00:02:39.627 --> 00:02:42.407
investigate failures when
edits share a document.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.644
We evaluated eight AI systems.

00:02:47.525 --> 00:02:51.977
Claude Code with Opus five achieved the
highest overall pass rate, eighty-seven

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:57.436
point one percent. Three human participants
averaged eighty-two point eight percent.

00:02:58.316 --> 00:03:03.303
The other seven AI systems scored between
forty-four point five and sixty-six point

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:09.451
seven percent. Every system scored lower on
combined-edit tasks than on single edits.

00:03:10.344 --> 00:03:14.630
The gaps ranged from three point two to
nineteen point one percentage points.

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:21.963
Consider this combined request: rotate the
middle rectangle ninety degrees, then align

00:03:21.976 --> 00:03:24.693
all three rectangles by
their visible top edges.

00:03:25.574 --> 00:03:28.380
Opus five gets the
rotation and sizes right.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.521
However, the visible top edges
do not align, so the task fails.

00:03:34.401 --> 00:03:39.401
Its stored frame tops line up, but rotation
shifts the visible edge relative to that

00:03:39.413 --> 00:03:44.146
frame. The same system passes each
edit when requested separately.

00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:48.766
Performance also varies
across Word features.

00:03:49.647 --> 00:03:52.657
Tables and paragraphs
lead, while pictures trail.

00:03:53.537 --> 00:03:57.453
Similar single-edit scores can hide
very different combined-edit scores.

00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:03.461
Gemini three point eight Flash and G L M
five point two both score about fifty-eight

00:04:03.474 --> 00:04:05.093
percent on single edits.

00:04:05.986 --> 00:04:10.604
Their combined-edit scores are forty-nine
percent and thirty-nine point six percent.

00:04:11.496 --> 00:04:16.369
Failures include valid documents with
incorrect edits, invalid documents that

00:04:16.382 --> 00:04:21.331
Word cannot open or that fail validation,
and cases where no file is submitted.

00:04:23.213 --> 00:04:28.341
WordEval makes the resulting document state
central to evaluation: it tests whether a

00:04:28.353 --> 00:04:33.149
request was carried out precisely, and
whether edits that work alone stay reliable

00:04:33.162 --> 00:04:38.916
together. The current tests use synthetic
documents and English instructions, and

00:04:38.928 --> 00:04:43.265
require Windows and Word, so scores
describe performance on these controlled

00:04:43.278 --> 00:04:45.484
tasks. Thank you.
